#
# Static analysis for the FoodOnTheGo backend.
#
# KI-003 recorded this as impossible from Module 01 and blamed the network
# egress policy. That diagnosis was wrong: the archives are refused because this
# development container's GitHub access is scoped to the project's own
# repository, and CI has never had that restriction at all. The tooling
# installed on the first attempt once the actual cause was known.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEVEL 3, AND WHY NOT 6
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# KI-003 named level 6. Level 6 is the goal and this is not it, so here is the
# measured ladder rather than an intention:
#
#     level 3   0 errors   <- the gate, enforced in CI
#     level 4  45 errors
#     level 5  50 errors
#     level 6 133 errors
#
# A gate is only a gate if it is green, and a gate set where the code does not
# yet pass is a gate somebody turns off in a hurry. Level 3 already refuses the
# things that matter most -- unknown methods and properties, wrong return types,
# a value assigned to a property that cannot hold it -- and every one of those
# it found on adoption was a real defect, including two the whole test suite
# could not see.
#
# What levels 4-6 add is almost entirely missing generics and array value types
# (`array` that could hold anything, `Collection` without its element type).
# That is genuine work worth doing and it is a lot of it; done badly, in a rush
# to reach a number, it produces `array<mixed, mixed>` everywhere and a codebase
# that is no better described than before. Raise the level when the annotations
# are real.
#
# No baseline file, deliberately. A baseline records "this was already broken
# when we started" and then never runs out. The findings above are written down
# in docs/13-known-issues.md where a person will read them, not in a file the
# tool reads and silences.
#
includes:
    - vendor/larastan/larastan/extension.neon

parameters:
    level: 3

    paths:
        - app
        - config
        - database
        - routes
        - tests

    # Tests are analysed too, deliberately. A test is the code that decides
    # whether everything else is correct, and a test with a wrong type is a test
    # that can assert the wrong thing confidently. Four of the defects found on
    # adoption were in tests.

    # THE TWO SETTINGS THAT MAKE THE ANALYSIS TRUE RATHER THAN NOISY.
    #
    # Without them Larastan cannot see an Eloquent attribute's type, so every
    # model property is unknown and every comparison against the enum a column is
    # cast to reads as "always false". The first run reported 434 errors; 210 of
    # them were that one blind spot wearing different hats.
    #
    # They are needed together and neither is enough alone. The migrations say
    # which columns exist; only the model says that `orders.status` is cast to an
    # OrderStatus. Adding the migrations by themselves changed the count from 434
    # to exactly 434 -- a control that stayed silent, which is how the second
    # setting was found.
    #
    # Migrations rather than a live database, so the analysis is reproducible on
    # a machine with no MySQL running, which is what the CI job is.
    databaseMigrationsPath:
        - database/migrations

    # This project declares casts in Laravel 11's `casts()` METHOD rather than
    # the older `$casts` property, and Larastan does not read that method unless
    # asked. The default is false.
    parseModelCastsMethod: true
